WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
How do you count animals when most stay hidden?

2
00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:07.280
One visit misses them, and a missing animal may not be dead.

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:13.400
Mark-recapture uses repeated sightings to estimate abundance, survival and

4
00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:14.400
occupancy.

5
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.280
Start in a closed pond, with no births, deaths, or animals leaving.

6
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.940
Catch and mark 50 animals, then release them to mix in.

7
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.400
Later, catch 90 and count the overlap: 20 already carry your mark.

8
00:00:30.433 --> 00:00:34.813
The marked share of the second catch estimates the marked share of the whole pond.

9
00:00:36.253 --> 00:00:41.073
Multiply first catch by second, then divide by marked recaptures: the

10
00:00:41.073 --> 00:00:42.433
Lincoln-Petersen formula.

11
00:00:43.853 --> 00:00:49.313
50 × 90 is 4,500; divided by 20 gives 225.

12
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.643
The plain estimate runs high with small samples.

13
00:00:55.023 --> 00:01:02.463
Chapman's version adds 1 to each count: (51 × 91) / 21 − 1 gives 220.

14
00:01:03.817 --> 00:01:08.817
That proportion works only if marks stay readable and every animal is equally

15
00:01:08.817 --> 00:01:14.717
catchable. Suppose marked animals learn to avoid traps and enter the second catch

16
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:18.937
less often. Will the estimate come out too high or too low?

17
00:01:24.467 --> 00:01:29.707
Too high: trap-shy marked animals shrink the overlap, so you divide by too little.

18
00:01:31.147 --> 00:01:34.127
Trap-happy animals do the reverse and shrink the estimate.

19
00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:41.527
Photo identification avoids handling, as in a 2018 Quito dog survey, but can

20
00:01:41.527 --> 00:01:42.607
mismatch individuals.

21
00:01:44.127 --> 00:01:46.627
Every result is an estimate, not a headcount.

22
00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:52.197
Over longer intervals, populations open: animals die or move.

23
00:01:53.517 --> 00:01:59.057
On repeated visits, an animal seen after a gap was alive and missed, separating

24
00:01:59.057 --> 00:02:00.457
detection from survival.

25
00:02:01.797 --> 00:02:03.957
Still, a survivor that leaves looks dead.

26
00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:10.817
Apparent survival is true survival times site fidelity: the chance a survivor stays

27
00:02:10.817 --> 00:02:16.797
in the area. Searching nearby sites and double-marking catch emigration and mark

28
00:02:16.797 --> 00:02:17.797
loss.

29
00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:22.090
Marking and revisiting unlocked findings single counts miss.

30
00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:28.390
On the Galapagos island Daphne Major, Boag and Grant banded and measured nearly

31
00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:30.210
every medium ground finch.

32
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:39.090
After the 1977 drought killed roughly 85 their 1981 report showed larger birds

33
00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:43.250
survived best. Males with larger beaks did best.

34
00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:48.070
Beak and body size go together, so the target trait is unclear.

35
00:02:49.233 --> 00:02:55.053
In 2006, Olendorf and colleagues recaptured marked male guppies in three Trinidad

36
00:02:55.053 --> 00:03:01.293
streams. They set morphs at about 3 common to 1 rare, reversing which pattern was

37
00:03:01.293 --> 00:03:09.093
rare. After 15 or 17 days, rare males had higher estimated survival, if missing fish

38
00:03:09.093 --> 00:03:10.513
died rather than moved.

39
00:03:11.633 --> 00:03:14.993
Zeros are ambiguous: absent, or just missed.

40
00:03:16.353 --> 00:03:22.173
In 2002, MacKenzie and colleagues analysed repeat Maryland wetland surveys from

41
00:03:22.173 --> 00:03:29.363
2000. American toads were detected at 10 of 29 wetlands, about 0.34.

42
00:03:29.363 --> 00:03:34.573
Modelling detection raised occupancy to 0.49.

43
00:03:36.073 --> 00:03:39.633
Repeated marks and visits separate missed animals from true loss.
